<code>assert \forall int index; 0<=index && index<=\old(this.size()); (object == null && this.get(index) == null) || (object != null && this.get(index).equals(object));</code>

However, the given comment seems to imply a condition about the state of the set after adding an object, not about the state of the set before adding an object. Let's refine the assertion to reflect that the added object should be at the end of the set if it was not already present.

Here is the corrected assert statement:

<code>assert \old(this.contains(object)) => \forall int index; 0<=index && index<=\old(this.size()); (object == null && this.get(index) == null) || (object != null && this.get(index).equals(object)) && !\old(this.contains(object)) => this.get(\old(this.size())) == object;</code>

But since the condition is about the state after adding the object, the correct assertion should be:

<code>assert \old(this.contains(object)) || this.get(\old(this.size())) == object;</code>

This ensures that if the object was not already in the set, it is now at the end of the set.

So the final answer is:

<code>assert \old(this.contains(object)) || this.get(\old(this.size())) == object;</code>
